<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-solve-rational-inequality">
  <title>Solving Rational Inequalities</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      You now know how to create a sign analysis summary of polynomials and rational expressions
      when they are written in factored form. This allows us to solve inequalities involving
      polynomials and rational expressions when they can be factored.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-polynomial-inequality-repeated-root" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: A polynomial inequality</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        When an expression is already factored and simplified, we know how to create
        a sign analysis summary number line:
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the root of each factor and mark it on a number line.
                These will be the end-points of our sign intervals.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Determine the sign inside each of the intervals. You can use test points with the
                expression for each interval or you can find one interval and then use even/odd power
                analysis to get the rest.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
      </p>
      <p>
        Solving an inequality which is comparing an expression to zero is exactly the same as
        identifying positive and negative intervals, just with an added interpretation step.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathrm{expression} &gt; 0</m> is finding the set of values where the expression is <em>positive</em>.
            </p>
            <p>
              <m>\mathrm{expression} \ge 0</m> is finding the set of values where the expression is <em>positive</em> and also any values where the expression <em>equals zero</em>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Similar ideas apply to <m>\mathrm{expression} &lt; 0</m> and <m>\mathrm{expression} \le 0</m>, except we are looking at sets where the expression is <em>negative</em>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-one-polynomial-inequality-repeated-root" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the inequality <m>\displaystyle (x+1)^2(x-3) &lt; 0</m> using the following steps.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>Create the number line summary of the sign analysis for the expression.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Because the inequality involves <q><m>&lt; 0</m></q>, identify all of the values on the sign summary that make the expression <em>negative</em>. Since this is a strict inequality, the roots themselves are not included.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Write the set using interval notation.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-polynomial-inequality-three-roots" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: A polynomial inequality with three roots</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now try the same process on a polynomial with three distinct linear factors, this time
        using an <q>or equals</q> inequality.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-two-polynomial-inequality-three-roots" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the inequality <m>\displaystyle x(x+2)(x-3) \ge 0</m> using the following steps.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>Create the number line summary of the sign analysis for the expression.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Because the inequality involves <q><m>\ge 0</m></q>, identify all of the values on the sign summary that make the expression <em>positive</em> or <em>equal</em> to zero. Since every factor is in the numerator, all three roots are included in the solution.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Write the set using interval notation.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-rational-inequality-simple" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 3: A rational inequality</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A rational expression introduces a small complication: a factor in the denominator gives a
        vertical asymptote instead of a root. It still marks the end of a sign interval on the
        number line, but it is <em>never</em> part of the solution set, even when the inequality
        includes <q>or equals</q>.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is essential that you pay attention to the difference between <term>roots</term> and <term>vertical asymptotes</term> (or other discontinuities) because the <em>or equals</em> part of an inequality only involves the roots, not the asymptotes.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-one-rational-inequality-simple" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the inequality <m>\displaystyle \frac{(x+2)(x-1)^2}{x-3} \ge 0</m> using the following steps.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>Create the number line summary of the sign analysis for the expression.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Because the inequality involves <q><m>\ge 0</m></q>, identify all of the values on the sign summary that make the expression <em>positive</em> or <em>equal</em> to zero.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Write the set using interval notation.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the inequality <m>\displaystyle \frac{(x+2)(x-1)^2}{x-3} \lt 0</m>.
          You already created the sign analysis summary for the same expression on the last problem.
          Only the interpretation is different.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the inequality <m>\displaystyle \frac{(x+2)(x-1)^2}{x-3} \le 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
